using Format, LinkTypes;

//======================================================================

readonly DocReference ProgramAnnouncementReference = new(#53db497e-a3bc-4d83-81e7-91c91dd026cc#, Article) {
  Title: "TongueTwister Program Announcement";
  AuthorLink to [People.Huelsenbeck_John, People.Goldstein_David, People.McCreight_Shawn];
};

//======================================================================

class ProgramAnnouncementContent: AuthoredContent {

  Constructor {
    
    super.Constructor("Program Announcement", ProgramAnnouncementReference);
    Description = "Description of the TongueTwister program";
    SetCorrespondant(People.Goldstein_David);
    Title = "TongueTwister: Analysis of cognate word forms in a Bayesian framework";
    Abstract = new ProgramAnnouncementAbstractSection;

    AddContent(new ProgramAnnouncementIntroductionSection);
  }
}

//======================================================================

class ProgramAnnouncementAbstractSection: Content {

  Constructor {

    super.Constructor("Abstract");
  }

    override GetBody(ContentStyle style) = Block {

    style.Par {
      TextHeight: 12pt;
      SpaceBefore: 18pt;
      SpaceAfter: 12pt;
      "TongueTwister is a program for examining the evolution of cognates as sequences of phonemic states under an event-based model of insertion, deletion, and substitution. ";
      "Parameters are estimated in a Bayesian framework with Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) used to numerically approximate posterior probability distributions for parameters. ";
      "Unlike traditional cognate-based phylogenetic analyses that rely on fixed morphological codings or treat gaps as missing data, TongueTwister implements the TKF91 model, allowing alignments to be treated as latent variables and marginalized over using MCMC. ";
      "This approach accommodates alignment uncertainty while pooling information across sites to estimate substitution dynamics. ";
      "TongueTwister supports a range of substitution models, from a simple equal-rates model to the highly parameterized general time-reversible model, and provides tools for both posterior inference and marginal likelihood estimation via stepping-stone sampling. ";
      "The program is intended as a research tool for exploring sequence-based models of language evolution and for evaluating competing evolutionary hypotheses within a coherent probabilistic framework.";
    };
  };
}

//======================================================================

class ProgramAnnouncementIntroductionSection: Content {

  Constructor {

    super.Constructor("Introduction");
  }

  override GetBody(ContentStyle style) = Block {

    style.Par {
      "Like biological species, languages evolve through descent with modification, as words change within communities and communities split into diverging traditions of speech. ";
      "The pattern of language divergence, and specifically, the order of divergences among languages, can be described by a tree-like diagram called a phylogeny. ";
      "Interestingly, linguists began constructing language trees at about the same time that biologists began reconstructing the Tree of Life. ";
      "More recently, linguists have taken advantage of tools developed in the evolutionary biology community to estimate trees using Bayesian inference. ";
      "In particular, they have adopted the morphological stochastic models implemented in software such as MrBayes and BEAST. ";
      "(Morphological models describe the evolution of anatomical characteristics of organisms.) ";
      "Cognate-words that share a common ancestral origin, akin to homologous structures in biology, are coded by the linguist into discrete states (0, 1, 2, ...). ";
      "The coded cognate data are then analyzed by the software using Bayesian statistical methods. ";
      "The output is a probability distribution of evolutionary trees conditioned on the cognate coding scheme, often accompanied by posterior distributions for the other parameters of the phylogenetic model. ";
    };

    style.Par {
      "The comparative method in linguistics treats cognates much like biologists treat anatomical or morphological data. ";
      "A biologist, through detailed study of the anatomy of a group, assigns discrete states to species for a given trait. ";
      "A study that includes vertebrates, for example, might include a character such as “presence or absence of hair,” with mammals coded as state 1 and all other vertebrates coded as state 0. ";
      "Similarly, a linguist codes a cognate observed in several languages using states that reflect similarities and differences among the words. ";
      "This procedure has been used to great success, allowing linguists to reconstruct language trees and estimate divergence times within a rigorous statistical framework. ";
    };

    style.Par {
      "The morphological models used in these analyses are continuous-time Markov models. ";
      "Such a model is fully specified by the set of states that the process can visit and the rates of change between all pairs of states. ";
      "These rates are arranged in a rate matrix, Q, in which the off-diagonal entries are positive and represent the rate of change from state i to state j (q_{ij}, i neq j), while the diagonal entries are defined so that each row sums to zero, q_{ii} = -(q_{i1} + q_{i2} + ldots + q_{ik}). ";
      "The morphological models implemented in MrBayes and BEAST are so-called $M_k$ models: for each cognate character with $k$ observed states, evolution is modeled as a continuous-time Markov process with $k$ states. ";
    };

    style.Par {
      "The M_k framework has several limitations. ";
      "First, one must condition on the absence of constant patterns. ";
      "Linguists (and biologists) typically do not code characters in which all languages share the same state, so likelihood calculations must be conditioned on such patterns being absent. ";
      "Second, because state labels are arbitrary—one can relabel all 0s as 1s and all 1s as 0s for a given cognate without changing its meaning—the model should impose symmetry on the rates so that the probability of the character is invariant to state flipping. ";
      "Third, because the same state label has different meanings across different cognates, information cannot be pooled across cognates to learn about the dynamics of language change. ";
    };

    style.Par {
      "An alternative approach is to code the information contained in a word in the same way that biologists treat DNA sequences: as a sequence of states. ";
      "In biology, a DNA sequence is a string of nucleotides drawn from the alphabet of nucleotides {A, C, G, T}. ";
      "Unlike morphological codings, one cannot arbitrarily relabel the states of a sequence without changing its meaning; invariant sites are common and informative; and one can learn about substitution dynamics by pooling information across sites. ";
      "In linguistics, the information in a word can be represented as a sequence of phonemes, one for each segment of the word. ";
      "The phoneme alphabet is typically much larger than that of DNA, which contains only four nucleotides (or 20 amino acids in proteins). ";
    };

    style.Par {
      "The main obstacle to treating cognates as phoneme sequences is alignment. ";
      "In biology, alignment establishes the fine-scale homology of the nucleotides. ";
      "The aligned DNA sequences are already assumed to be homologous, using criteria such as sequence similarity, genomic context and synteny, and phylogenetic coherence; the alignment procedure establishes which nucleotides from one sequence are homologous to the nucleotides of the other sequences. ";
      "In biology, homologous DNA sequences are aligned using algorithms that maximize similarity among long sequences. ";
      "These procedures are more reliable when the sequences are long, because they can use conserved regions of the sequences to anchor the alignment. ";
      "Words, however, typically contain only a handful of segments, so alignment is highly uncertain. ";
      "The most principled way to account for this uncertainty is by marginalization: parameter estimates are averaged over all possible alignments, with each alignment weighted by its probability under the model. ";
    };

    style.Par {
      "A natural way to achieve such marginalization is to use a model of sequence evolution that allows sequences to grow or shrink through the insertion or deletion of segments over time. ";
      "The TKF91 model, introduced by Thorne et al. (1991), is precisely such a model. ";
      "It describes a historical process that allows a sequence to evolve through three types of events: substitution of one segment for another, deletion of a segment, or insertion of a new segment. ";
      "Under the TKF91 model, probability calculations can account for any alignment pattern. ";
      "When combined with Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC), the model allows exploration of alternative evolutionary histories while naturally marginalizing over alignment uncertainty. ";
    };

    style.Par {
      "The TongueTwister program, described here, implements the TKF91 model in a fully Bayesian framework, using MCMC to numerically approximate the posterior probability distribution of the model parameters. ";
    };
  };
}

//======================================================================
